      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Multiple factors are involved in the mechanism of bone cancer pain. Treatment with a single drug is not adequate to target all of the different mechanisms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To study the analgesic effect of a combination of low-dose dexmedetomidine (DEX) and tramadol (TRA) on bone cancer pain in rats."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A randomised, controlled study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Central Laboratory of Shanghai Chest Hospital, Shanghai Jiaotong University, Shanghai China, from July 2011 to June 2012."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "ANIMALS",
          "content": "Adult female Wistar rats weighing 180 to 200g."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Rats with bone cancer were divided into five groups based on drug treatment (n=12 for each group): T5 group (TRA 5 mg kg), T10 group (TRA 10 mg kg), D1 group (DEX 1 μg kg), T5+D1 group (TRA 5 mg kg+DEX 1μg kg) and IS (isotonic saline 0.5 ml) group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "The mechanical threshold and spontaneous paw withdrawal were measured in all groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Both the T5+D1 group and T10 group showed a significantly increased mechanical threshold and a lower incidence of spontaneous paw withdrawal compared with the IS group. Interestingly, there was no significant difference between the T5+D1 and T10 groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "We found that a combination of DEX and TRA at low doses provided equal or superior analgesic effects on bone cancer pain compared to high-dose TRA alone. Our animal data might indicate the clinical administration of these two drugs in bone cancer pain therapy."
        }
      ]},
